{"name": "bounded_depth_costly_speaker", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: bounded_depth_costly_speaker: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "Listeners interpret referring expressions with bounded reasoning depth, inverting an informative speaker who balances literal informativeness against utterance extension costs. Refining bounded_depth_listener by incorporating utterance extension costs (from isolated_graded_costly_l3) into the communicative speaker: speakers penalize words with broad contextual extensions, favoring more specific descriptions when available. This addresses severe empirical over-allocation to multi-feature singleton competitors and under-allocation to minimal targets when words are shared across objects. Differences from bounded_depth_listener: recursion depth remains unchanged (depth 1, listener calls L1); parameter cost_weight is added with prior dist.Normal(0.0, 1.0); no parameters are removed (retaining alpha and w_singleton); and the speaker choice utility subtracts cost_weight times the utterance extension cost (fraction of display objects satisfying each real utterance).", "context": "round 3 candidate 5 refine incumbent bounded_depth_listener repair 1"}
{"name": "literal_pragmatic_mixture", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: literal_pragmatic_mixture: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "Listeners vary in their communicative reasoning depth: the population is a mixture of literal listeners who interpret referring expressions according to literal semantic truth, and pragmatic listeners who invert an informative speaker. Communicative speakers choose words by expecting this heterogeneous population of listeners. On uninformative displays without an informative word, visual attention defaults to contextually unique singletons.", "context": "round 4 candidate 1 explore"}
{"name": "causal_diagnostic_listener", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: causal_diagnostic_listener: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "Listeners interpret referring expressions through causal diagnostic inference rather than recursive mental simulation, attributing a heard word to the referent that maximizes causal power relative to the counterfactual production baseline across display alternatives. Speakers communicate by selecting words that reliably distinguish the intended target from contextual competitors. On uninformative displays without an informative word, visual attention defaults to contextually unique singletons and feature contrast.", "context": "round 4 candidate 2 explore"}
{"name": "communicative_regret_speaker", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: communicative_regret_speaker: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "Speakers choose referring expressions by balancing communicative recovery against the regret of omitting a more informative description available for the target. Listeners invert this regret-averse speaker, attributing underspecified descriptions to minimal referents that lack distinguishing alternatives rather than multi-feature singletons. On displays where a heard word is shared between duplicate minimal targets and multi-feature singletons, this mechanism predicts listeners will strongly favor the minimal targets because the speaker would regret omitting the singletons' distinguishing features, disagreeing sharply with models whose visual singleton biases inflate belief in the complex competitor.", "context": "round 4 candidate 3 explore"}
{"name": "bounded_depth_feature_parsimony", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "admitted", "hypothesis": "Listeners interpret referring expressions with strictly bounded reasoning depth (depth 1) by inverting an informative speaker over literal semantics, but penalize candidate referents possessing redundant visual features using a feature parsimony heuristic taken from feature_parsimony_listener. On informative trials, this parsimony penalty prevents visual singleton salience from spuriously overriding communicative informativeness for multi-feature competitors, while on uninformative displays visual attention defaults to contextually unique singletons. Differences from bounded_depth_listener: recursion depth remains depth 1 (listener calls L1); parameter beta is added with prior dist.LogNormal(0.0, 1.0); no parameters are removed (retaining alpha and w_singleton); L1 adds feature_count to its signature and scales referent choice weights by exp(-beta * vec(feature_count, r)); and listener passes beta and ctx.feature_count into L1 on informative trials.", "context": "round 4 candidate 4 refine incumbent bounded_depth_listener"}
