{"name": "rsa_l1_shared_perceptual_prior", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: rsa_l1_shared_perceptual_prior: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "Listeners interpret referring expressions by inverting an informative speaker who models a foundational listener sharing common ground of perceptual salience, so that visual salience directly informs communicative expectations rather than being applied solely as a downstream selection bias. When hearing a word that applies universally across a scene, the speaker's expectation that the listener will naturally attend to visually unique singletons prevents a spurious M-implicature penalty against the singleton, correctly aligning predicted ratings with human responses on duplicate displays. Refining rsa_l1_singleton_feat_color_valence_l0 by making the contextual perceptual prior common knowledge in the foundational simulated listener L0: recursion depth remains depth 1 (listener calls L1); no parameters are added or removed (retaining alpha, w_singleton, w_distinct, w_features, w_valence, and w_color); L0 adds the prior vector to its signature and weights referents by the product of the prior vector and semantic truth; and in L1 the speaker evaluates L0 with the prior argument.", "context": "round 1 candidate 5 refine incumbent rsa_l1_singleton_feat_color_valence_l0"}
{"name": "bounded_depth_graded_truth", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: bounded_depth_graded_truth: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "Listeners interpret referring expressions with strictly bounded reasoning depth (depth 1) by inverting an informative communicative speaker, but evaluate semantic applicability as graded truth that diminishes with each additional competing visual feature. Refining bounded_depth_listener by incorporating graded semantic applicability from isolated_graded_costly_l3 into the literal semantics: candidate referents with additional unmentioned visual features possess lower baseline applicability, enabling listeners to strongly favor minimal referents when words are universally shared while maintaining visual singleton salience on uninformative displays. Differences from bounded_depth_listener: recursion depth remains depth 1 (listener calls L1); parameter gamma is added with prior dist.Normal(0.0, 1.0); no parameters are removed (retaining alpha and w_singleton); literal lexicon applicability is scaled by exp(-gamma * (feature_count - 1)) for real utterances; and the listener passes this graded lexicon into L1 on informative trials.", "context": "round 1 candidate 6 refine chosen"}
