{"name": "topic_discriminability_listener", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "admitted", "hypothesis": "Listeners interpret referring expressions by modeling what speakers are likely to talk about before an utterance is heard: a referential prior over objects grounded in communicative discriminability. Before hearing a word, listeners expect speakers to choose topics that can be successfully distinguished in context, assigning prior probability to each candidate referent according to the maximal literal communicative recovery of its features. On uninformative displays or universal utterances, this topic prior directs belief toward uniquely discriminable objects without relying on spurious visual singleton heuristics that distort scalar implicatures.", "context": "round 2 candidate 1 explore retry 1"}
