{"name": "semantic_coverage_listener", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: semantic_coverage_listener: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "Listeners interpret referring expressions through semantic information coverage, weighting candidate referents by the proportion of the object's total contextual feature information explained by the heard word. When a speaker uses a shared or underspecified description, referents possessing unmentioned distinguishing features are sharply discounted because cooperative speakers do not leave highly informative properties unsaid. On uninformative displays without an informative word, visual attention defaults to contextually unique singletons.", "context": "round 1 candidate 1 explore"}
{"name": "description_exhaustivity_listener", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: description_exhaustivity_listener: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "Listeners interpret referring expressions through description exhaustivity, assuming that cooperative speakers use single words to exhaustively describe minimal referents and applying a discrete non-exhaustivity penalty to any candidate referent possessing unmentioned features regardless of how many additional features it has. Communicative speakers choose expressions by anticipating this exhaustivity-sensitive listener, while spontaneous visual attention on uninformative displays defaults to contextually unique singletons. On multi-level scalar hierarchy displays containing minimal, intermediate, and maximal candidates, this mechanism predicts that listeners reject intermediate competitors almost as completely as maximal competitors because both violate description exhaustivity, disagreeing sharply with feature-uncertainty models that scale smoothly with feature count and over-predict intermediate choices.", "context": "round 1 candidate 2 explore"}
{"name": "contrastive_alternatives_speaker", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: contrastive_alternatives_speaker: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "Communicative speakers select referring expressions by penalizing avoidable ambiguity: candidate words are discounted proportionally to the excess competitors they fail to eliminate compared to the most specific alternative available for that referent. When an object cannot be uniquely named, the speaker communicates without penalty using the most informative description available. Pragmatic listeners invert this ambiguity-sensitive speaker, attributing underspecified words to referents that lack more specific alternatives, while spontaneous visual attention on uninformative displays defaults to contextually unique singletons.", "context": "round 1 candidate 3 explore"}
{"name": "feature_uncertainty_distinct", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: feature_uncertainty_distinct: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "Refining feature_uncertainty_listener_2 by incorporating continuous contextual distinctiveness from rsa_l1_singleton_feat_color_valence_l0 into the visual attention prior: listeners interpret referring expressions under referential feature uncertainty over literal semantics, but visual attention weights candidate referents by combining discrete singleton salience with continuous feature contrast relative to display competitors. This directly accounts for the contextual uniqueness of unmentioned features in scalar competitor displays and scales singleton salience in large competitor sets, addressing empirical discrepancies in competitor selection and singleton salience. Communicative speakers select expressions anticipating this feature-attribution inference, while spontaneous visual attention on uninformative displays follows the combined perceptual prior. Differences from feature_uncertainty_listener_2: recursion depth remains depth 1 (listener calls L1); parameter w_distinct is added with prior dist.Normal(0.0, 1.0); no parameters are removed (retaining alpha and w_singleton); the object prior is extended by adding w_distinct times contextual distinctiveness (summed pairwise Hamming distance across context objects for real features); memo definitions (L0 and L1) remain unchanged; and listener passes the extended prior into L1.", "context": "round 1 candidate 4 refine incumbent feature_uncertainty_listener_2"}
{"name": "feature_uncertainty_distinct_listener", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: feature_uncertainty_distinct_listener: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "Listeners interpret referring expressions under referential feature uncertainty, weighting literal semantic compatibility inversely by a referent's total visual feature count, while communicative speakers select expressions expecting this feature-attribution inference. Spontaneous visual attention weights candidate referents by combining discrete singleton salience with continuous contextual distinctiveness (feature contrast relative to all display competitors), addressing empirical failures to account for contextually unique unmentioned features and under-prediction of singleton salience in redundant or large competitor sets. Differences from feature_uncertainty_listener_2: recursion depth remains unchanged (depth 1, listener calls L1); parameter w_distinct is added with prior dist.Normal(0.0, 1.0); no parameters are removed (retaining alpha and w_singleton); and the visual prior is extended by adding w_distinct times the continuous contextual distinctiveness (summed Hamming feature distance across display objects).", "context": "round 1 candidate 5 refine incumbent feature_uncertainty_listener_2"}
