/scratch/users/mcfrank/auto-psych/agent_trees/e6344f63e5fb0046/repo/_runs/chain_0/experiment3/loop/attempted_hypotheses.jsonl:
  Line 1: {"name": "grammatical_exhaustification", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "admitted", "hypothesis": "People resolve referring expressions using grammatical exhaustification over unasserted alternative descriptions rather than recursive Theory of Mind. When hearing a word, listeners rule out referents proportionally to the informativeness of any truer or more specific features of that referent that the speaker left unmentioned. On uninformative trials, visual attention defaults to contextually unique singletons.", "context": "round 1 candidate 1 explore"}

  Line 13: {"name": "contextual_oddity_listener", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "admitted", "hypothesis": "People interpret referring expressions by integrating communicative informativeness with contextual oddity salience. Before hearing an utterance, visual attention is captured by a solitary odd-one-out that breaks the homogeneous background of duplicate distractors, establishing a default referential prior over objects. Pragmatic listeners invert an informative speaker using this oddity-grounded referent prior, defaulting to uniform expectations when multiple distinct items or no oddities exist.", "context": "round 2 candidate 1 explore"}

  Line 14: {"name": "bounded_depth_listener", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "admitted", "hypothesis": "Listeners interpret referring expressions with a strictly bounded reasoning depth, inverting a communicative speaker who reasons about literal semantics rather than engaging in higher-order recursive mental simulation. This bounded depth prevents over-implicature, naturally preserving non-zero probability for multi-feature scalar competitors while strongly favoring minimal referents when words are universally shared. On uninformative displays without an informative word, visual attention defaults to contextually unique singletons.", "context": "round 2 candidate 2 explore"}

  Line 17: {"name": "grammatical_exhaustification_distinct", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "admitted", "hypothesis": "Refining grammatical_exhaustification by incorporating continuous contextual distinctiveness into the visual attention prior: listeners interpret referring expressions through grammatical exhaustification over unasserted alternative features, ruling out referents proportionally to the informativeness of any more specific descriptions the speaker omitted, while spontaneous visual attention weights referents by combining discrete singleton salience with continuous feature contrast (contextual distinctiveness) relative to display competitors. This directly improves the representation of displays lacking identical duplicates as well as empty singletons in full-duplicate contexts, addressing the empirical discrepancy where humans allocate substantial prior belief to empty singleton objects among identical full-feature duplicates. Differences from grammatical_exhaustification: recursion depth remains unchanged (depth 0, L_exh); parameter w_distinct is added with prior dist.Normal(0.0, 1.0); no parameters are removed; and the visual prior is extended by adding w_distinct times the total contextual feature contrast (summed Hamming distance across all context objects).", "context": "round 2 candidate 6 refine chosen"}

  Line 19: {"name": "rsa_l1_singleton_feat_color_valence_l0", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "admitted", "hypothesis": "RSA pragmatic listener at depth 1 with singleton salience, contextual distinctiveness, visual feature complexity, evaluative valence framing, visual color contrast, and literal L0. Refining rsa_l2_singleton_feat_color_valence_l0 by reducing pragmatic recursion depth from depth 2 to depth 1: listeners interpret referring expressions by inverting an informative speaker who evaluates literal semantics, rather than engaging in higher-order recursive mental simulation. This bounded depth eliminates over-implicature in scalar implicature and prevents multiplicative prior compounding across reasoning tiers, preserving appropriate probability on multi-feature competitors while maintaining common knowledge of visual singleton salience, continuous contextual distinctiveness, valence-modulated feature complexity, and color contrast. Differences from rsa_l2_singleton_feat_color_valence_l0: recursion depth is reduced from depth 2 to depth 1 (listener calls L1 instead of L2), the depth-2 listener memo L2 is removed, and all parameters (alpha, w_singleton, w_distinct, w_features, w_valence, w_color) are preserved without addition or removal.", "context": "round 2 candidate 4 refine incumbent rsa_l2_singleton_feat_color_valence_l0 retry 1 repair 1"}

  Line 21: {"name": "feature_parsimony_listener", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "admitted", "hypothesis": "Listeners interpret referring expressions using a feature parsimony heuristic: among candidate referents satisfying a heard word, listeners choose the object possessing the fewest total visual features, assuming speakers provide minimal descriptions for simple targets rather than leaving distinguishing features unmentioned. Speakers communicate by selecting words that guide this parsimony-biased listener to the intended referent. On uninformative displays without an informative word, visual attention defaults to contextually unique singletons.", "context": "round 3 candidate 1 explore"}

  Line 22: {"name": "structured_lapse_listener", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "admitted", "hypothesis": "People separate communicative inference from response generation: listeners infer communicative intent using an unbiased, uniform target prior, and resolve their response through a salience-guided structured lapse that captures attention toward contextually unique visual singletons. On uninformative displays lacking a communicative word, choices default entirely to visual singleton salience; on informative trials, communicative beliefs are mixed at the decision level with the salience lapse rather than multiplied inside Bayes' rule.", "context": "round 3 candidate 2 explore"}

  Line 24: {"name": "bounded_depth_unbiased_prior", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "admitted", "hypothesis": "Refining bounded_depth_listener by decoupling visual singleton salience from communicative inference: listeners interpret informative referring expressions with bounded reasoning depth under an unbiased, uniform referent prior, confining visual singleton salience strictly to spontaneous visual attention on uninformative displays without an informative word. This prevents spurious singleton bonuses from overriding scalar implicatures on informative trials, eliminating severe over-allocation to multi-feature singleton competitors when words are shared with duplicate minimal targets. Differences from bounded_depth_listener: recursion depth remains unchanged (depth 1, listener calls L1); no parameters are added or removed (retaining alpha and w_singleton); the prior vector parameter is removed from L1 so that the listener chooses referents proportionally to speaker choice probabilities alone; and listener calls L1 without the prior argument, restricting the singleton prior exclusively to prior trials.", "context": "round 3 candidate 4 refine incumbent bounded_depth_listener"}

  Line 31: {"name": "bounded_depth_feature_parsimony", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "admitted", "hypothesis": "Listeners interpret referring expressions with strictly bounded reasoning depth (depth 1) by inverting an informative speaker over literal semantics, but penalize candidate referents possessing redundant visual features using a feature parsimony heuristic taken from feature_parsimony_listener. On informative trials, this parsimony penalty prevents visual singleton salience from spuriously overriding communicative informativeness for multi-feature competitors, while on uninformative displays visual attention defaults to contextually unique singletons. Differences from bounded_depth_listener: recursion depth remains depth 1 (listener calls L1); parameter beta is added with prior dist.LogNormal(0.0, 1.0); no parameters are removed (retaining alpha and w_singleton); L1 adds feature_count to its signature and scales referent choice weights by exp(-beta * vec(feature_count, r)); and listener passes beta and ctx.feature_count into L1 on informative trials.", "context": "round 4 candidate 4 refine incumbent bounded_depth_listener"}

  Line 62: {"name": "topic_discriminability_listener", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "admitted", "hypothesis": "Listeners interpret referring expressions by modeling what speakers are likely to talk about before an utterance is heard: a referential prior over objects grounded in communicative discriminability. Before hearing a word, listeners expect speakers to choose topics that can be successfully distinguished in context, assigning prior probability to each candidate referent according to the maximal literal communicative recovery of its features. On uninformative displays or universal utterances, this topic prior directs belief toward uniquely discriminable objects without relying on spurious visual singleton heuristics that distort scalar implicatures.", "context": "round 2 candidate 1 explore retry 1"}

  Line 69: {"name": "feature_uncertainty_listener_2", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "admitted", "hypothesis": "Listeners interpret referring expressions under referential feature uncertainty, being unsure which visual feature of a candidate referent the speaker intended to pick out. When evaluating literal semantics, listeners weight an object's compatibility by the probability that the speaker chose the heard feature from that object's feature bundle, naturally favoring minimal referents whose features are exhaustively described over cluttered multi-feature competitors. Communicative speakers select expressions expecting this feature-attribution inference, while spontaneous visual attention on uninformative displays defaults to contextually unique singletons.", "context": "round 2 candidate 3 explore repair 1"}
